-- title:
--   Finite spanning set for the integral closure of A₀[j]
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (the chosen model of the algebraic closure of $\mathbb Q$) and an intermediate field $K$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$ that is finite-dimensional over $\mathbb Q$. Write $A_0 = A \cap K$ for the subring `coeffSubring A K` of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, let $j =$ `jqModC` be the Laurent series $\mathsf q^{-1}\cdot(\text{the integral power series } \mathtt{jNum})$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q}(\!(\mathsf q)\!)$, let `jRing A K` be the subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}(\!(\mathsf q)\!)$ generated by the constant series with coefficients in $A_0$ together with $j$, and let `fieldOver (1*q) K` be the subfield generated by the constant series with coefficients in $K$ together with $j$ and `jqNModC` at level $1\cdot q$. Let $C =$ `jIntegralClosure (1*q) A K` be the subring of elements of that subfield which are integral over `jRing A K`. The assertion is that there exists a finite subset $s$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}(\!(\mathsf q)\!)$ with $s \subseteq C$ such that every $x \in C$ admits a function $f$ on Laurent series with $f(c) \in$ `jRing A K` for all $c \in s$ and $x = \sum_{c \in s} f(c)\,c$; that is, $s$ spans $C$ over `jRing A K`.
--
--   This is the finiteness of the integral closure of $A_0[j]$ in the function field $K(j(\mathsf q), j(\mathsf q^{q}))$, phrased as the existence of a finite spanning set inside the Laurent series field so that no algebra instance on the subrings is needed. It feeds the corresponding module-finiteness and integrality statements for the localisation at a node in the $\lambda$-level situation.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_NodeLocalized_exists_finset_forall_mem_jIntegralClosure_eq_sum_mul.lean

